<LegalLayout title="Support - Walkable: Walks &amp; Places" description="Support and help for the Walkable: Walks &amp; Places iOS app.">
  <p class="eyebrow">Walkable: Walks &amp; Places</p>
  <h1>Support</h1>

  <p>Walkable records your walks, even with your phone locked, and shows you a map of your route, your stats, and 3 to 5 short facts about the places you passed. There is no account and no setup; just tap Start.</p>

  <h2>Common questions</h2>

  <h2>Do I need an account?</h2>
  <p>No. There is no sign-up and no login. Just open the app and tap Start.</p>

  <h2>How does it record my walk with my phone locked?</h2>
  <p>Walkable keeps recording your location in the background while a walk is running, even with the screen off or your phone in your pocket, and shows the elapsed time on your lock screen and Dynamic Island.</p>

  <h2>What stats do I get?</h2>
  <p>Distance, duration, moving time, pace, speed, steps, cadence, elevation, and splits for every walk. If an Apple Watch recorded your heart rate and active energy during the walk, those are shown too, read from Apple Health.</p>

  <h2>What are the facts about places?</h2>
  <p>After a walk, Walkable shows 3 to 5 short facts about places you passed, drawn from Wikipedia, Wikidata, OpenStreetMap, and, in New South Wales, the NSW Heritage Register. Each fact is credited to its source. Where available, facts are rewritten in plain language on your device using Apple&rsquo;s on-device models; the facts themselves always come from these public sources, never invented.</p>

  <h2>Why does a stretch of my walk show as a dashed line?</h2>
  <p>If part of a walk looks like it was covered by train or bus, Walkable leaves that stretch out of your walking stats and shows it as a dashed line on the map, so your pace and distance reflect your walking.</p>

  <h2>What if I forget to stop recording?</h2>
  <p>If a walk has been recording with no movement for a while, Walkable can send you a local notification to remind you, in case you left it running by accident.</p>

  <h2>Does it need internet access?</h2>
  <p>Recording a walk and seeing your route and stats works without internet. Finding facts about nearby places and the weather strip both need an internet connection at the time.</p>

  <h2>What if I say no to a permission it asks for?</h2>
  <p>Walkable asks for location access to record your route, motion access to read your steps, and notification access for the forgotten-stop reminder; Apple Health access is also optional. If you say no to location, Start opens your iPhone&rsquo;s Settings so you can turn it back on. Other permissions can be turned on later in Settings &gt; Walkable, and Apple Health access in the Health app under Sources or Apps.</p>

  <h2>Is my data collected?</h2>
  <p>No personal information is collected. Walkable has no account, no ads, and no analytics. Once a walk ends, the app sends the finished route&rsquo;s coordinates to public services to find places and facts (Wikipedia, Wikidata, OpenStreetMap, and the NSW Heritage Register); separately, while the app is open, it sends your current location to Open-Meteo for the weather strip. Your route is also shown using Apple Maps. Nothing that identifies you is sent with any of these. See our <a href="/walkable/privacy">Privacy Policy</a> for details.</p>

  <h2>Where is my data stored?</h2>
  <p>Your walks, routes, stats, and facts are stored only on your iPhone, in the app&rsquo;s own local database. If you save a walk, it can also be written to Apple Health as a walking workout with its route, if you allow Apple Health access.</p>

  <h2>How do I delete a walk?</h2>
  <p>Open your walk history and delete the walk you no longer want. This removes it and its route from the app. Deleting the app itself removes all of its data from your phone.</p>

  <h2>Need more help?</h2>
  <p>Email us at <a href="mailto:support@mikhailnarbekov.com">support@mikhailnarbekov.com</a>. We aim to reply within a few business days.</p>
